# Binary formats are stored and checked out byte for byte.
#
# Without this file git guessed text or binary from content, and ReportLab
# writes the built guides in docs/ as 7-bit ASCII (every stream ASCII85
# encoded), so they were taken for text. With core.autocrlf=true a checkout
# turned each LF into CRLF, moving every byte offset the xref table records:
# a fresh clone's guides were refused by pypdf as "Broken xref table".
#
# Text line endings are deliberately not set here. They stay with
# core.autocrlf as before. Every text blob in the index is LF already, so
# `* text=auto` would rewrite nothing stored, but it would change what a
# Windows clone with autocrlf=false checks out (CRLF where it gets LF today),
# and that wants its own change with eol=lf for the deploy scripts.

# Git for Windows gives PDFs and Word files a text diff through pdftotext in
# its system gitattributes (diff=astextplain). `binary` on its own would
# switch that off; where no such driver exists git uses its own diff.
*.pdf binary diff=astextplain
*.docx binary diff=astextplain
*.doc binary diff=astextplain
*.xlsx binary
*.xls binary
*.pptx binary

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.bmp binary
*.webp binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary

*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary

*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.7z binary

*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.mkv binary
*.webm binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary

*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
